window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-a5bf923361999e85"]={"key":"ny_main-a5bf923361999e85","group":"ny_main","uid":"a5bf923361999e85","rank":79,"title":"np stakeholder referral briefing 001","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are a nurse practitioner supporting occupational-health communications for Tidewater Field Services and Kilnbrook Industries at Sandpiper Terminals. During the week of 14 October, Linnea Arriaga asked you to revise a draft presentation after Emeka Ibarra challenged its comparison of first-aid referrals in September and October. The Hartsmere Board is waiting to choose between refresher training and a revised triage process, and one unusual incident involved possible anaphylaxis; reviewers disagree about whether that incident belongs in the general trend narrative. Please create an approval-ready presentation for these cross-functional internal stakeholders by 16 October at noon. It must explain what can and cannot be concluded about the material change between the two periods, address the disagreement about the unusual incident, and recommend exactly one of the two options for the Board to consider. The draft deck and underlying referral records are not attached, so do not turn the missing evidence into a guessed count, rate, diagnosis, cause, outcome, policy threshold, cost, or effectiveness claim; make the occupational-knowledge gap and its effect on the comparison plain. Keep patient details out of the presentation and keep it focused on stakeholder communication rather than patient-specific clinical orders or implementation instructions. The ordinary referral comparison and the rare but potentially severe incident both need to be handled without allowing one concern to erase the other, and the deck should make the resulting judgment understandable to readers who are not clinicians. Use no more than eight slides and no more than 650 visible words, with readable labels and a coherent briefing pace suitable for about seven minutes. End with an unambiguous decision statement rather than a tie between the options. Write the single file `first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx` into the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx` — An approval-ready internal stakeholder presentation comparing September and October first-aid referrals, handling the possible anaphylaxis exception, and presenting one defensible option for Board consideration. (Must be a PowerPoint presentation.; Must contain no more than eight slides.; Must contain no more than 650 visible words.; Must not invent quantitative results, clinical diagnoses, patient details, policy requirements, costs, outcomes, or source citations.; Must be suitable for a cross-functional internal audience and readable when rendered.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx","bytes":42526,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/086068c7b85db666493fba2b1fca90c6bd167b2c4e91b38b4cd226e564bac45f.pptx","sha256":"086068c7b85db666493fba2b1fca90c6bd167b2c4e91b38b4cd226e564bac45f","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nOCCUPATIONAL-HEALTH BRIEFING | DECISION REQUEST\nSeptember vs October\nFirst-Aid Referral Comparison\nFor the Hartsmere Board and cross-functional internal stakeholders\nSituation\nLinnea Arriaga requested a revision after Emeka Ibarra challenged the September-to-October comparison.\nThe Board is weighing refresher training versus a revised triage process.\nBriefing deadline: 16 October, noon.\nBOARD CHOICE\nChoose one option. Do not treat missing records as a trend result.\nSandpiper Terminals\nTidewater Field Services + Kilnbrook Industries\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nEVIDENCE STATUS\nThe comparison is challenged because its evidence is unavailable\nKNOWN\nSeptember and October are the requested periods. One unusual incident was described as possible anaphylaxis.\nThe supplied description does not establish diagnosis, cause, outcome, or trend.\nNOT SUPPLIED\nUnderlying September and October referral records; the draft comparison; and the local first-aid referral definition for validation.\nEmeka Ibarra's challenge is a valid evidence-and-interpretation issue.\nConclusion: a material change cannot be established or quantified from the supplied material. Missing records are not evidence of zero referrals, no change, an increase, or a decrease.\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nCOMPARISON DISCIPLINE\nA defensible period comparison needs like-for-like evidence\nMEASURE\nCount is not a rate\nCount: referrals recorded in a period.\nRate or proportion: needs a common exposure basis, such as workforce, work hours, shifts, or first-aid visits.\nMATCH\nMatch the periods\nComparable coverage and exposure.\nThe same referral definition and classification.\nConsistent repeat or duplicate encounter handling.\nOBTAIN\nEvidence still needed\nPeriod-specific records and encounter dates.\nReferral classification and definition.\nCoverage or exposure denominator basis.\nWithout these inputs, an apparent difference could reflect unequal coverage, exposure, or classification rather than an operational change.\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nEXCEPTION HANDLING\nKeep the rare safety signal visible without turning it into the routine trend\nROUTINE LENS\nOrdinary first-aid referral trend\nUse validated, like-for-like records to describe routine patterns. That conclusion is not currently supportable.\nSAFETY LENS\nPossible anaphylaxis\nA possible serious allergic reaction. It remains unconfirmed here; no diagnosis, cause, patient detail, or outcome is claimed.\nJudgment: report the possible anaphylaxis incident separately from the routine comparison until classification is validated. A rare event can be highly significant because potential consequence and urgency matter, even when frequency does not define the trend.\nEffect: protects routine comparability and preserves a high-consequence safety concern; it does not prove a period change or favor an option.\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nBOARD OPTIONS\nBoth options address different parts of the communication problem\nAssessment lens: routine referral consistency and visibility of rare, potentially high-consequence presentations.\nOPTION A\nRefresher training\nPOTENTIAL BENEFIT\nCould reinforce recognition and more consistent referral handoffs.\nMATERIAL LIMITATION / CONDITION\nDoes not by itself create a shared routing structure or repair unavailable comparison evidence.\nOPTION B\nRevised triage process\nPOTENTIAL BENEFIT\nTriage means a consistent routing and classification structure; it could separate serious presentations for attention.\nMATERIAL LIMITATION / CONDITION\nRequires local governance, validation, and adoption; no effect can be claimed before evidence is reviewed.\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nRECOMMENDATION + OPEN EVIDENCE\nRecommendation for Board consideration: revised triage process\nWHY THIS OPTION\nAn evidence-aware operational choice\nCan distinguish routine referral routing from a separate safety signal without declaring a trend.\nKeeps the possible anaphylaxis concern visible without treating it as evidence of option effectiveness or clinical outcome.\nThis is professional interpretation, not a measured result.\nOUTSTANDING\nEvidence validation remains open\nACCOUNTABLE\nLinnea Arriaga, with the records owner\nBY 16 OCTOBER, NOON\nObtain or validate September and October records, definition/classification, and coverage or exposure basis.\nStatus: outstanding; not yet verified.\nBoard consideration only - no approval, implementation, or patient-care instruction is represented.\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\nDECISION STATEMENT\nBoard decision requested\nFOR BOARD CONSIDERATION\nA revised triage process.\nReason: it preserves a separate lane for a potentially high-consequence possible anaphylaxis incident while keeping routine referrals comparable once the missing evidence is validated.\nBounded conclusion: a material September-to-October change cannot be established from the supplied material.\nThis recommendation does not claim a diagnosis, cause, outcome, policy requirement, or measured effect.","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共7页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is present under the exact filename first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"File name and extension in the working directory.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level directory for the exact filename; do not award this item for a differently named or nested file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PowerPoint opens successfully and renders as a coherent presentation without corrupt or missing slide content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening and rendering the PPTX.","grader_hint":"Open with an offline presentation tool and render all slides. Award only if the file is usable rather than merely having a valid extension."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than eight slides.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Slide count in the PPTX.","grader_hint":"Inspect the slide collection; count hidden slides as slides if present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The visible slide text is no more than 650 words.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Extracted or manually counted visible text.","grader_hint":"Count text rendered on slides, excluding document metadata and speaker notes. Use a consistent whitespace-delimited count and allow a small counting variance only where punctuation or numerals make tokenization ambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck has no materially clipped, overlapping, unreadably small, or off-slide content.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered slide images.","grader_hint":"Review every rendered slide at normal presentation proportions. Penalize only material defects that impede reading or interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies its purpose as a September-versus-October first-aid referral comparison and communicates that a Board choice is required.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title or opening framing text.","grader_hint":"Look for the two periods, first-aid referrals, and a decision or option-selection purpose in visible content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation is clearly addressed to the Hartsmere Board and cross-functional internal stakeholders rather than to patients or clinicians alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience labels, framing, terminology, and decision context.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a non-clinical stakeholder can tell why the material matters to the Board and what decision context it supports."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck makes its approval or briefing deadline clear, including 16 October at noon or an unambiguous equivalent.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visible date or deadline statement.","grader_hint":"Accept a clearly stated 16 October noon deadline or a plainly equivalent same-deadline expression."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation has a coherent instructional briefing sequence that moves from context and evidence status through interpretation and options to a decision statement.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-deck order and transitions.","grader_hint":"Assess whether an audience can follow the logic without having to reconstruct why the evidence, exception, and options are being discussed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Clinical terminology is translated or contextualized sufficiently for cross-functional readers, while the deck remains concise.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions, plain-language explanations, and visible labels.","grader_hint":"A non-clinical Board member should understand the distinction between a routine referral trend, triage, and a possible anaphylaxis event without asking the author."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening accurately identifies the operational situation: a challenged comparison of first-aid referrals across September and October and a pending choice between refresher training and a revised triage process.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening slide or executive framing.","grader_hint":"Verify that the deck reflects the supplied situation rather than introducing a different operational problem."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"September and October are explicitly identified as the comparison periods.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period labels in text or table.","grader_hint":"Accept full month names or unambiguous Sep/Oct labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck does not silently assume an arbitrary local definition of first-aid referral; it either gives a defensible operational definition or explicitly marks the definition as unavailable for validation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definition, scope note, or limitation statement.","grader_hint":"Award when the deck makes clear what event population is being discussed or acknowledges that the source definition is missing. Do not require a particular invented code or threshold."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck distinguishes referral counts from referral rates or proportions rather than treating those measures as interchangeable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison language, table, or explanatory text.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear statement that a count alone is not a rate and that the denominator or exposure basis affects interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison identifies the exposure or denominator needed to interpret a period change, such as workforce, hours, shifts, visits, or another explicitly qualified operational basis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data limitation or measurement section.","grader_hint":"Accept any defensible denominator appropriate to the setting, provided it is presented as required evidence rather than as a fabricated value."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation states that the underlying September and October referral records and the draft comparison evidence were not supplied or are unavailable for verification.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-status or limitations text.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit statement about absent source records, not merely a vague claim that data quality is limited."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The absence of records is not represented as evidence that there were zero referrals, no change, or a known increase or decrease.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative and qualitative trend claims.","grader_hint":"Inspect the deck for the epistemic distinction between unavailable evidence and an observed zero or unchanged result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation contains no fabricated counts, percentages, rates, patient cases, dates of individual incidents, outcomes, costs, or effect sizes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All displayed numbers and factual claims.","grader_hint":"Numbers used only for the stated deadline or slide/word constraint are acceptable. Any operational or clinical result presented as observed must be traceable to the brief; no such result was supplied."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck explicitly states whether a material change between September and October can be established and, if it cannot, explains that the missing source evidence prevents a defensible conclusion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trend conclusion or limitations statement.","grader_hint":"A valid conclusion is that material change cannot presently be quantified or confirmed. Do not require a direction of change that the inputs cannot support."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies the principal evidence needed to resolve the comparison, including period-specific referral records and an appropriate exposure or denominator basis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-gap or evidence-requirements content.","grader_hint":"Accept a concise list of defensible fields or evidence categories such as dates, unique encounters, referral classification, and exposure volume. Do not require a private schema."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation recognizes that September and October must be compared on a like-for-like basis and avoids treating unequal coverage, exposure, or classification as a genuine operational change.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison caveats and interpretation.","grader_hint":"Look for awareness of period length, staffing or exposure differences, and category consistency without demanding any particular adjustment formula."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck acknowledges Emeka Ibarra's challenge as a legitimate evidence or interpretation issue rather than dismissing it or presenting the challenged comparison as settled fact.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Narrative framing of the review disagreement.","grader_hint":"Award when the challenge is represented fairly and connected to what the available evidence can support."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The unusual incident is described as a possible anaphylaxis event, preserving the uncertainty in the supplied description.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incident text or label.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording such as suspected or possible anaphylaxis, but not an unqualified confirmed diagnosis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The possible anaphylaxis incident is visibly distinguished from the routine first-aid referral trend rather than being silently merged into or omitted from the general narrative.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visual grouping and explanatory text.","grader_hint":"The distinction may be made through a dedicated section, callout, or clearly labeled treatment, provided the audience can see that it is an exception."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck explains why a rare event can have high operational or clinical significance independent of its frequency.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception interpretation.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit recognition of consequence or urgency, not merely the adjective rare or severe in isolation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation makes an explicit, intelligible judgment about how the possible anaphylaxis incident relates to the general trend narrative and describes the interpretive consequence of that judgment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception-handling conclusion.","grader_hint":"Accept either inclusion with a clear caveat or separate treatment with a clear reason. The item requires a visible trade-off, not a hidden or contradictory treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck does not convert the possible anaphylaxis description into a confirmed diagnosis, confirmed causal mechanism, or claim about patient outcome.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clinical wording throughout the deck.","grader_hint":"Inspect qualifiers and surrounding claims. Occupational-health communication may describe the concern, but cannot assert unsupported clinical certainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation contains no patient-identifying information or invented details that could identify the individual involved.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All visible text, charts, and examples.","grader_hint":"Award when the incident is discussed at an appropriately de-identified, aggregate level."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck remains a stakeholder briefing and does not provide patient-specific treatment orders, individualized medical advice, or unsupported emergency-management claims.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clinical content and scope statements.","grader_hint":"General recognition of the need for urgent clinical assessment is acceptable; detailed instructions for treating a particular patient are outside scope."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation distinguishes clinical-safety significance from the separate operational decision between refresher training and a revised triage process.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Linkage between exception and options.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear boundary: the incident informs the operational discussion but is not itself presented as proof that one operational option produced a clinical result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both refresher training and a revised triage process are explicitly presented as the two options under consideration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Options or decision framing.","grader_hint":"Accept close equivalents, but both alternatives must be identifiable and not replaced by an invented third category."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The options are assessed against criteria relevant to both routine referral operations and the rare, high-consequence exception.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Option comparison and rationale.","grader_hint":"Examples of relevant criteria include consistency, recognition or escalation of serious presentations, usability, and evidence requirements. Do not require a specific scoring framework."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states a plausible benefit and a material limitation or condition for refresher training without claiming an unsupported measured effect.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Training-option discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept occupationally defensible qualitative trade-offs, such as reinforcing recognition or consistency while not necessarily changing the triage structure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states a plausible benefit and a material limitation or condition for a revised triage process without claiming an unsupported measured effect.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Triage-option discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept occupationally defensible qualitative trade-offs, such as improving routing or escalation while requiring governance, adoption, or validation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation makes the edge-case trade-off explicit: a simple, comparable routine-trend narrative may conflict with preserving visibility of a rare but severe safety signal.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trade-off statement or integrated interpretation.","grader_hint":"The audience should be able to identify both competing legitimate requirements and see why the possible anaphylaxis incident tests the ordinary treatment of trend data."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation names exactly one recommendation between the two stated options.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement.","grader_hint":"Either option is acceptable. Do not award if the deck recommends both, presents a tie, or substitutes an unrequested third option."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is supported by an explicit rationale that connects the choice to the available facts, the data limitation, and the rare-exception trade-off.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"A defensible rationale may be qualitative and uncertainty-aware; it must not depend on invented September or October results."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is presented consistently wherever it appears in the deck, with no contradictory option named in the summary, comparison, or closing statement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-slide comparison of recommendation wording.","grader_hint":"Compare executive framing, option discussion, and final statement. Treat materially different labels as inconsistency even if one can be inferred from context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck does not use unsupported costs, staffing requirements, legal conclusions, policy thresholds, causal explanations, or effectiveness percentages to make the recommendation appear more certain.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and supporting claims.","grader_hint":"General professional reasoning is acceptable; factual claims about local policy, measured impact, or financial effect require supplied evidence, which is absent here."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Observed facts from the brief, professional interpretation, assumptions, and unknowns are distinguishable in the presentation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, caveats, and wording of claims.","grader_hint":"Look for clear epistemic signposting such as known, cannot verify, interpretation, assumption, or evidence gap. Do not require those exact words if the distinction is unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies a concrete accountable role or stakeholder and a date for obtaining or validating the missing evidence, while clearly labeling that work as outstanding rather than completed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-gap ownership and timing statement.","grader_hint":"Accept a named stakeholder from the brief or a clearly stated accountable role, paired with a specific date or deadline. Do not accept a claim that records were already checked."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck makes clear that the recommendation is for Board consideration and does not falsely claim Board approval, implementation, or completed operational change.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision-status wording.","grader_hint":"The presentation may recommend an option, but must not represent the pending choice as already authorized or implemented."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The closing content leaves the audience with a concise, unambiguous decision statement rather than an unresolved list of alternatives.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final slide or final visible statement.","grader_hint":"The last substantive message should identify one option and its bounded rationale. A separate short caveat about evidence limitations is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck does not present unsupplied jurisdictional policy, regulatory requirements, or local clinical thresholds as established facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Policy and standards language.","grader_hint":"Because no policy text or jurisdictional source was supplied, award only when such claims are omitted or clearly framed as items requiring local confirmation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation is usable as an instructional briefing: its main distinctions are memorable, its transitions support comprehension, and a stakeholder can understand the decision without asking the author to decode the deck.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-deck instructional and communication quality.","grader_hint":"Judge practical usability for a seven-minute cross-functional briefing, including whether the deck teaches the difference between a missing trend record and a safety-significant exception while retaining a clear decision focus."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an occupational-health briefing for cross-functional leaders who need a careful period comparison and a bounded decision communication despite the absence of underlying referral records. The presentation must preserve the distinction between routine operational trends and a rare, high-consequence clinical exception.","difficulty_sources":["Transforming a data-free occupational-health brief into a concise, audience-specific decision presentation.","Maintaining an explicit evidence boundary when the requested period comparison cannot be numerically verified.","Handling a rare, potentially severe clinical exception without folding it uncritically into a routine operational trend.","Making one bounded recommendation while preserving the distinction between Board consideration, operational judgment, and patient-specific clinical care."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":[],"gold_construction":{"source_basis":"Occupational knowledge only. Build the gold presentation from the brief and ordinary nurse-practitioner communication practice; do not create a source workbook or invent referral observations.","known_facts":["The setting is Sandpiper Terminals and the stakeholder organizations are Tidewater Field Services and Kilnbrook Industries.","Linnea Arriaga requested a revision after Emeka Ibarra challenged a September-versus-October first-aid referral comparison.","The Hartsmere Board is waiting to choose between refresher training and a revised triage process.","One unusual incident involved possible anaphylaxis, and reviewers disagree about whether it belongs in the general trend narrative.","The underlying referral records and draft presentation are not available."],"required_epistemic_boundary":["Do not represent absent records as zero referrals, no change, or a measured increase or decrease.","Do not invent counts, rates, denominators, incident details, patient outcomes, policy thresholds, costs, staffing assumptions, or effectiveness estimates.","State that a material change cannot be defensibly established from the supplied material and identify the evidence needed for validation.","Separate possible anaphylaxis from routine trend interpretation while explaining the trade-off between comparability and preservation of a high-consequence signal.","Preserve the word possible and avoid confirming a diagnosis or outcome."],"professional_content":["Explain the difference between a raw count and a rate or proportion and identify the exposure or denominator needed for a valid period comparison.","Flag like-for-like issues such as period coverage, exposure, classification, duplicate or repeat encounters, and the need for a consistent referral definition, without inventing a local data dictionary.","Explain that a rare event may carry substantial safety significance even when it should not be allowed to distort an ordinary trend without explicit treatment.","Present the two requested operational options with qualitative benefits, limitations, and implications for both routine workflow and serious-event recognition.","Make one option the recommendation for Board consideration, clearly marked as an evidence-aware operational recommendation rather than an implemented decision or patient-care order. Either option is acceptable if the rationale is defensible.","Identify an accountable stakeholder or role and a concrete date for validating the missing evidence, labeling this as outstanding work rather than completed verification."],"instructional_quality":["Use a concise progression from purpose and audience, through evidence status and comparison logic, to the exception, options, recommendation, and closing decision statement.","Explain clinical terms for cross-functional readers and keep the deck suitable for an approximately seven-minute briefing.","Keep all text within the requested eight-slide and 650-visible-word limits and ensure the rendered deck is readable."]},"gold_derivation":"Create a compact presentation with no quantitative answer key. Validate it against every rubric predicate by rendering it offline, checking the slide and visible-word limits, confirming that all claims are either in the brief or explicitly qualified professional interpretation, and verifying that the recommendation is singular and repeated consistently. A gold that chooses revised triage process may use the possible high-consequence exception as part of its qualitative rationale, but it must not claim that the incident proves a trend or diagnosis; a gold choosing refresher training must explain why that choice remains defensible despite the exception and must preserve the exception as a separate safety concern."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"revised_referral_briefing","filename":"first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"An approval-ready internal stakeholder presentation comparing September and October first-aid referrals, handling the possible anaphylaxis exception, and presenting one defensible option for Board consideration.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be a PowerPoint presentation.","Must contain no more than eight slides.","Must contain no more than 650 visible words.","Must not invent quantitative results, clinical diagnoses, patient details, policy requirements, costs, outcomes, or source citations.","Must be suitable for a cross-functional internal audience and readable when rendered."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"professional_report","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use an occupational-health or nurse-practitioner reviewer for clinical framing and an independent presentation reviewer for evidence discipline, audience fit, rendering, and decision communication. The reviewers should assess the submitted deck itself and should accept either named option when the rationale is explicit, internally consistent, and grounded in the supplied facts and acknowledged uncertainty.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No referral records, draft presentation, denominator, or local policy text are supplied, so a gold presentation must not contain a factual September-to-October count or rate.","The correct professional handling is to distinguish what can be concluded from what cannot be verified; the rubric therefore does not require a particular direction of trend or a particular one of the two options.","The possible anaphylaxis incident is intentionally described without enough information to confirm diagnosis or outcome."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:01:27+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:01:27+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:01:27+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:01:27+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/a5bf923361999e85","declared_deliverables":["first_aid_referral_comparison_briefing.pptx"]};
